Q: C17 vs C23: () function pointer error - Is this the correct fix? A: <p>It is true that the behavior of empty parenthesis changed with C23. Writing functions with &quot;non-prototype&quot; format (ie no parameter types) has however been flagged as an obsolescent feature for some ~35 years so we had plenty of time to adapt.</p>
<p>Since your main concern is portability:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>(Function) pointer to integer/integer to (function) pointer conversions are implementation-defined.</strong> Most obviously because the address bus on various systems differ in size, but in theory the internal formats of the pointers could differ between systems too.</p>
<p>In C17 or older, signed integer formats could also have one out of 3 allowed forms. This matters too since some exotic systems use negative numbers as significant (ie negative is kernel space).</p>
<p>Whereas unsigned integers do not have this portability problem and could still be used in exotic systems with negative addresses, since MSB of the unsigned number then becomes significant, rather than positive/negative value.</p>
<p>This means that signed integer types are less portable than unsigned integer types for the purpose of storing pointer/address values.</p>
<p>The most portable type for storing a pointer as integer is supposedly &amp; according to the C rationale <code>uintptr_t</code>, but this type too is strictly speaking optional, and also not portable to C89. However in practice, I have yet to see a C99 or later compiler for any target that didn't implement <code>uintptr_t</code>.</p>
<p><strong>What this means in practice:</strong></p>
<p><code>uintptr_t</code> is more portable than <code>intptr_t</code> and any are far more portable than <code>uint64_t</code> which in turn is more portable than <code>QWORD</code>, which is a name that collides with non-portable Windows type.</p>
<p>Use <code>uintptr_t</code> - if not supported then create a similar <code>typedef</code>, which is generally how C89 compatibility is handled for all types in <code>stdint.h</code>.</p>
<p>Better yet do not perform <em>any</em> integer-to-pointer/pointer-to-integer conversions at all, for maximum portability. There is no reason in this case to do that. Where you do arithmetic on an integer you might as well do array indexing of the array of function pointers, which would also have improved readability. Non-obfuscated could would simply take <code>MyCallBacks[slot]</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Casting from one function pointer to another is ok, but calling the function afterwards when the function types are non-compatible is explicitly undefined behavior.</strong> It is fine to use a generic pointer type like <code>void(*)(void)</code> for storing any function pointer, but you must always convert back to the correct function pointer type before calling the function. Meaning that for this to work in a complex scenario, you would have to store the type information somewhere.</p>
<p><strong>What this means in practice:</strong></p>
<p>For a maximum portable, type-generic program, you will have to maintain a list of all supported types, for example in an <code>enum</code> variable. This would also enable some tricks like union type punning, but the obvious down-side is that you need to maintain a list of every function type ever to be used.</p>
<p>We may quickly note that &quot;not knowing the callbacks prototype&quot; is an artificial and burdensome requirement. There is no practical context where callbacks are used and we do not know the format. Rather, program designs using callbacks <em>force</em> you to use a certain format, no matter if it is <code>bsearch</code>, some embedded system HAL or some PC thread library.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Another option is something like <code>typeof(MyCallBackA)* MyCallBacks[] = { &amp;MyCallBackA, &amp;MyCallBackB };</code>. This assumes that you don't know the types of the callbacks but at least you know that they are of the same type. This is C23 compliant but not compatible with C17. However, it is supported by <code>-std=gnu17</code> or older still. Not compatible with older MSVC but then that compiler has severe standard compliance problems anyway and will not accept a lot of strictly conforming C programs.</p>
<p>Also notable, there is no sane or semi-professional coding style out there accepting the use of function pointers without <code>typedef</code>.</p>
<hr />
<p>I would perhaps have rewritten your program like this instead:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;

uint64_t MyCallbackA (uint64_t a, 
                      uint64_t b, 
                      uint64_t c, 
                      uint64_t d)
{
    return a+b+c+d;
}

uint64_t MyCallbackB (uint64_t a, 
                      uint64_t b, 
                      uint64_t c, 
                      uint64_t d)
{
    return a+b+c+d;
}


typedef uint64_t MyCallback_t (uint64_t a, uint64_t b, uint64_t c, uint64_t d);
typedef void GenericCallback_t (void);

static GenericCallback_t* const MyCallbacks[] = 
{ 
    (GenericCallback_t*)&amp;MyCallbackA, 
    (GenericCallback_t*)&amp;MyCallbackB 
};

int main(void)
{
    int slot = 1;
    MyCallback_t* ptr = (MyCallback_t*)MyCallbacks[slot];
    return ptr(1, 2, 3, 36);
}

</code></pre>
<p>Since the code also removed the questionable to/from integer conversions and arithmetic, there's actually no reason to use <code>uintptr_t</code> either.</p>
